  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 1015 PM CDT Sat Oct 10 2026\n\nMonday through Saturday:\n\nOblong low pressure spanning from the High Plains to the\nUpper Mississippi River Valley regroups northeastward on Monday, \ndrifting into WI. Warm / moist advection ahead of this system \nyields some low chances (~10-20%) for showers in the daytime hours\nMonday. The 10.18z GFS allows a shortwave trough to crest the\nridge midday as well, though that stands as an outlier for now. \nThe increasing heat and humidity (near record high temps and near\nrecord warm low temps for MSN, dewpoints entering the low 60s) \nwill bring increasing levels of instability with it, sufficient \nfor some thunderstorms (~1,500 joules CAPE on most model \nguidance). That said, shower and storm chances remain fairly low\nuntil Monday evening / Monday night when the core of the low \npressure system crosses central / south-central WI (20-45% \nprecip chances). An isolated stronger storm cannot be ruled out \nas the low crosses the region, but severe weather appears \nunlikely at this time. Hail (elevated convection) would be the \nmain concern if applicable. \n\nThe cold front associated with this system crosses our region\nTuesday morning, bringing northwest winds and dry weather in \nbehind it. Predominantly dry weather expected midweek, though \nensembles hint at a stalled baroclinic zone just south of the \nstate, yielding some additional 10-30% rain chances on \nWednesday, mainly towards the IL border. There is plenty of \ndisagreement among model guidance for the late half of the work \nweek, particularly pertaining to the presence of / intensity of \na clipper-system that could yield additional rain showers \nThursday into Friday (40-50% chances until things become \nclearer). \n\nThe general consensus is then for strong high pressure to descend\nfrom western Canada into the Dakotas late this week, eventually\ndrifting overhead at some point this weekend. This should lead to\nstrong CAA, much colder temperatures, and frost potential for our\nregion. If the aforementioned Thursday/Friday clipper system \nwinds up being present, the pressure gradient between the incoming\nhigh pressure and departing clipper system could drive some very \ngusty northwest winds late this week. \n\nSheppard",
